-- help-account-rm.txt --
Log an account out and move its dir to the trash.

--purge deletes the dir permanently instead and removes every record squadrn keeps of the
account: its usage record, its handoffs, its in-use records and its cached login status.
At a terminal it asks you to type the account (e.g. claude/personal), even with --yes;
without one it needs --purge --yes.

Usage:
  srn account rm <account> [flags]

Flags:
  -f, --force   remove even if a session seems to use the account
  -h, --help    help for rm
      --purge   delete the dir permanently and every record squadrn keeps of the account
  -y, --yes     do not ask for confirmation (with --purge: only without a terminal)

Global Flags:
  -a, --account string   account to run as (provider/name or name)
  -C, --dir string       run as if started in this directory
      --no-color         disable colors (also: NO_COLOR=1)
-- help-usage.txt --
Show what the status line last reported about each account's usage limits (5-hour and
7-day windows), when it last hit a limit, and the best account to continue with in this repository.

Usage is recorded by the status line, so it needs a one-time setup per account:
srn usage setup wraps the status line command (your own one keeps running and showing).

Usage:
  srn usage [<account>] [flags]
  srn usage [command]

Examples:
  srn usage                 all accounts
  srn usage work            details of claude/work
  srn usage setup --all     record usage for every account
  srn usage setup --undo    restore the previous status line

Available Commands:
  setup       Record an account's usage through its status line

Flags:
  -h, --help      help for usage
      --json      print JSON
      --refresh   query login status, plan and organization again
      --watch     redraw every 5 seconds

Global Flags:
  -a, --account string   account to run as (provider/name or name)
  -C, --dir string       run as if started in this directory
      --no-color         disable colors (also: NO_COLOR=1)

Use "srn usage [command] --help" for more information about a command.
